<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">African National Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ANC, South African Native National Congress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The African National Congress is a South African political party founded in 1912 as the South African Native National Congress to defend the rights of Black South Africans. It led opposition to apartheid, was banned in 1960 and turned to armed struggle, and its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> was imprisoned until 1990. It has governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> since the first democratic elections of 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/african-national-congress.jpg" alt="African National Congress" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: African National Congress Vector: UberHalogen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_African_National_Congress.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the same time the Cape’s non-racial qualified franchise and its mission schools, such as Lovedale, produced the first generation of educated African leaders, among them the journalist John Tengo Jabavu, who influenced the early <b>African National Congress</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…massacre of 21 March 1960 had been followed by a state of emergency and the banning of the <b>African National Congress</b> and the Pan Africanist Congress, and in December 1961 the ANC and the South African Communist Party launched an armed wing, Umkhonto we Sizwe, with Nelson Mandela…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tanzania’s main military commitment was to the liberation of southern Africa: Dar es Salaam hosted the Liberation Committee of the Organisation of African Unity, and FRELIMO of Mozambique, the <b>ANC</b> of South Africa and other movements trained in Tanzanian camps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Organised resistance also began under the Union: the <b>South African Native National Congress</b>, renamed the African National Congress in 1923, was founded at Bloemfontein in 1912; Clements Kadalie’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Educated Africans founded the <b>South African Native National Congress</b>, later the African National Congress (ANC), in 1912 to petition for their rights, and among Indians Mahatma Gandhi led campaigns of passive resistance against discriminatory laws between 1906 and 1914.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>African National Congress</b> (ANC) regarded it as offensive because of its link with apartheid and minority rule, and it was agreed that it would not be sung at rugby matches; yet at a test match against New Zealand in 1992 the crowd sang it during a silence…</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li></ul></section>
